    "answer": {
      "short": "The least element $d$ of the positive integer linear combinations divides both $a$ and $b$, and every positive common divisor divides $d$. Hence $d=\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)=am+bn$ for some $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\nUse the division algorithm and the minimality of $d$ to show that the remainders are zero.",
      "worked": "Let $S=\\{ax+by:x,y\\in\\mathbb Z\\text{ and }ax+by>0\\}$. Since $a,b$ are not both zero, either $|a|$ or $|b|$ is a positive member of $S$. Thus $S$ has a least element $d=am+bn$ for some $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\nBy the division algorithm, $a=dq+r$, where $q,r\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $0\\le r<d$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}r&=a-dq\\\\r&=a-q(am+bn)\\\\r&=a(1-qm)+b(-qn).\\end{align*}$$\nIf $r>0$, then $r\\in S$, contradicting the minimality of $d$. Therefore $r=0$, so $d\\mid a$. Applying the same argument to $b$ gives $d\\mid b$.\n\nLet $f$ be any positive common divisor of $a$ and $b$, with $a=fu$ and $b=fv$ for integers $u,v$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}d&=am+bn\\\\d&=fum+fvn\\\\d&=f(um+vn).\\end{align*}$$\nThus $f\\mid d$, so $f\\le d$. Since $d$ is itself a positive common divisor, $d=\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)$. Consequently $am+bn=\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)$, and this is the smallest positive integer of the required form.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
